Some problems I see at the first glance:  

- Many, many, many conditionals.  Wear "oLS or rather "oR?  
  When to wear conflict?  

- Movement is not even trivial for shopkeepers, who 
  don't perform complicated tasks like maximizing 
  their survival probability. 

- Many things can't be expressed easily in survival probability.  
  How good/valuable is it to have a scroll called DUAM SKNAHT 
  identified?  How valuable is a wish?  Again, that's all 
  highly context-sensitive, so even more conditionals appear.  
  As a consequence, deciding about gains/losses of an action 
  may not be trivial.  

It would be highly interesting to have a learning algorithm 
which makes guesses the one or the other way.  However, this 
also seems to be complicated, since it would have to generalize, 
but not to overgeneralize.  And to generalize 'into the 
right direction'.  

Example:  @ is hit by Orcus' death ray while wearing "oLS.  
" crumbles, ray bounces, hits again, @ dies. 

Experience:  In this exact game situation (37.382 moves, 298 HP, 
killed 93 orcs, etc, etc, etc), "oR would have yielded a better 
outcome than "oLS.  

Too little generalization:  When meeting Orcus, wear an "oR. 

Good generalization:  Always try to get reflection.  

Over-generalization:  Always prefer "oR over "oLS.

